  "answer": "**Not really. Inflation has re-accelerated over the past year and is above the RBA's 2–3% target band, so the RBA has been raising rates again.**\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026, up from 1.9% in June 2025. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and eased to 3.5% in July before rising again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 2.8% in June 2025. It has risen for most months since late 2025 and has been at or near 3.6% since June 2026.\n- **Weighted median:** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 3.1% in June 2025.\n- **Cash rate:** The RBA cut to 3.6% by late 2025, then raised it from February 2026. It reached 4.35% in June 2026 and was 4.36% in September 2026.\n\nThe underlying measures are the better guide, since headline inflation swings with volatile items like fuel and energy. Both trimmed mean and weighted median have been around 3.4–3.6% for most of 2026, well above target. Inflation was closer to control in mid-2025, when the trimmed mean was around 2.8–3.0%, but it is not now.",
